//
// @SUBJECT: closures
// @CLAIM: capture is copy-at-definition and same-scope capture works.  The limits are real
//         and are decisions: a mutated scalar may be captured by exactly ONE closure, and a
//         capturing closure cannot be stored in a collection.
// @SEEN-FROM: 00-vs-rust.html#closures · 00-vs-python.html#closures
// @RATIONALE: DESIGN_DECISIONS.md C38 (copy-at-definition), C74 (one closure per mutated
//         scalar), C75 (closure-carrying struct values are frame-bound), C115, C116
//
// Rust:   let add = |b| a + b;             // borrows or moves, checked
// Python: add = lambda b: a + b           # captures the CELL, so a later `a = …` is seen
//
// The Python difference is the one asserted first: loft copies at DEFINITION, so a later
// write to the captured variable is not seen by the closure.  Python's late binding is the
// classic loop-variable surprise; loft does not have it, and that is the trade for not being
// able to observe a caller's later state.

fn apply(f: fn(integer) -> integer, v: integer) -> integer { f(v) }

fn test_capture_is_copy_at_definition() {
  a = 10;
  add_a = fn(b: integer) -> integer { a + b };
  a = 100;                       // after the definition
  assert(apply(add_a, 1) == 11, "the closure kept the value `a` had when it was made (C38)");
  assert(a == 100, "and the variable itself moved on");
}

fn test_same_scope_capture_works() {
  base = 5;
  scale = 3;
  f = fn(v: integer) -> integer { (v + base) * scale };
  assert(apply(f, 1) == 18, "two same-scope captures, read together");
}

// C116: a collection element holds a plain fn-ref, never a capturing closure.  That is the
// limit the page states, and a reader who hears only "closures cannot go in a vector"
// concludes too much — the table of plain functions works, and is asserted here.
//
// ⚠ Written with `for` and a bare index on purpose, and that is now the PERMANENT answer
// rather than a workaround.  The obvious spelling — `fs[0] ?? double`, which a C80 reader
// reaches for because an element read is nullable — was loft#1642: it panicked the interpreter
// out of range (`fn_call_ref: d_nr=… is negative`, a raw Rust message) and did not compile on
// `--native`, while `if g != null` on a fn-ref was REFUSED.  Found by this file on its first
// run.
//
// The owner ruled that a function type stays NON-nullable (`(N-Opt)` unchanged), so the two
// spellings agree by REFUSING together: `f ?? g` on a fn-ref is a compile error like
// `f != null`.  An out-of-range read is the ABSENT function (`closures.md` `(L-FnAbsent)`),
// and calling it is a C80 fault answering the return type's null — no panic, so C66 holds.
// The two cures are the ones below: an index check, or the struct wrapper
// (`(acts[k] ?? fallback).f(x)`) that `LUA_BAR.md` LC1 records as the real keyed idiom, where
// the `??` discharges a RECORD and the fn-ref never has to be nullable.
//
// So do NOT restore the `??` here when loft#1642 lands — it is refused by design.
fn test_a_plain_fn_ref_is_what_a_collection_holds() {
  fs: vector<fn(integer) -> integer> = [double, negate];
  got: vector<integer> = [];
  for f in fs { got += [apply(f, 4)]; }
  assert(len(got) == 2, "two functions in the vector");
  assert((got[0] ?? 0) == 8, "the first is `double`");
  assert((got[1] ?? 0) == -4, "the second is `negate`");
  assert(apply(fs[0], 4) == 8, "and a bare index reaches one directly");
}

fn double(v: integer) -> integer { v * 2 }
fn negate(v: integer) -> integer { -v }
